1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:16,189
In the spring of 1945, Berlin was a ruin. Soviet artillery was falling on the government quarter, and fifteen metres underground, in a concrete bunker, the man who had started it all was running out of time.

2
00:00:17,289 --> 00:00:23,092
Twelve years earlier, he had been handed power legally, by the president of a democracy.

3
00:00:24,192 --> 00:00:28,437
In those twelve years, his regime murdered six million Jews.

4
00:00:29,537 --> 00:00:36,684
And it started a war in Europe that helped make the Second World War the deadliest conflict in human history.

5
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:48,515
Adolf Hitler is the name the world reaches for when it wants to describe evil. But he did not do it alone, and he did not do it all at once. This is how it happened.

6
00:00:50,715 --> 00:01:01,915
Adolf Hitler was born on the twentieth of April, 1889, in Braunau am Inn, a small Austrian town on the border with Germany.

7
00:01:03,015 --> 00:01:13,809
His father was a customs official with a temper. His mother, Klara, doted on him. As a teenager he drifted out of school and dreamed of becoming an artist.

8
00:01:14,909 --> 00:01:25,747
In 1907, and again in 1908, the Academy of Fine Arts in Vienna turned him down. His mother died of cancer in between.

9
00:01:26,847 --> 00:01:36,553
For the next five years he lived in Vienna on very little, at times in a men's hostel, selling small paintings of city scenes to get by.

10
00:01:37,653 --> 00:01:48,405
Vienna was a city full of nationalist pamphlets and a mayor who used hatred of Jews to win votes. Hitler later claimed this was where his worldview formed.

11
00:01:50,605 --> 00:01:58,883
In 1913 he moved to Munich. When war broke out the next year, he volunteered for the Bavarian army.

12
00:01:59,983 --> 00:02:08,751
He served for four years as a regimental dispatch runner, carrying messages near the front lines, and was awarded the Iron Cross.

13
00:02:09,851 --> 00:02:20,112
In October 1918 he was temporarily blinded in a gas attack. He was in a hospital bed when he heard that Germany had surrendered.

14
00:02:21,212 --> 00:02:39,985
The peace treaty that followed took territory from Germany, limited its army, and demanded huge reparations. Many Germans believed a lie: that their army had not really lost, but had been stabbed in the back by traitors at home. Hitler believed it, and he blamed Jews and communists.

15
00:02:42,185 --> 00:02:54,153
Back in Munich, the army sent him to watch a tiny political group called the German Workers' Party. He joined it instead. He discovered he had one great talent. He could hold a crowd.

16
00:02:55,253 --> 00:03:05,003
The party renamed itself the National Socialist German Workers' Party, the Nazis. By 1921, Hitler was its leader.

17
00:03:06,103 --> 00:03:18,433
In November 1923, he tried to seize power by force in Munich. The coup collapsed in a single day. Sixteen of his followers and four police officers were killed.

18
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:31,416
He was convicted of treason and sentenced to five years. He served about nine months, in comfortable conditions, and used the time to dictate a book: Mein Kampf, My Struggle.

19
00:03:32,516 --> 00:03:44,911
The book set out almost everything he would later try to do. A world of racial struggle. Conquest of living space in the east. And Jews cast as the enemy behind every problem.

20
00:03:46,011 --> 00:03:52,219
When he got out, he changed tactics. He would not storm power again. He would win it through elections.

21
00:03:54,419 --> 00:04:05,939
In 1928 the Nazis won less than three percent of the vote. Then came the Great Depression. Millions of Germans lost their jobs, and the extremes grew.

22
00:04:07,039 --> 00:04:17,876
By July 1932, the Nazis were the largest party in parliament, with about thirty-seven percent. They never won a majority in a free election.

23
00:04:18,976 --> 00:04:31,264
Conservative politicians believed they could use him and control him. On the thirtieth of January 1933, President Hindenburg appointed Hitler chancellor.

24
00:04:32,364 --> 00:04:43,393
Four weeks later, the parliament building burned. Hitler used the fire as a pretext. Civil liberties were suspended, and thousands of political opponents were arrested.

25
00:04:44,493 --> 00:04:52,344
In March, the Enabling Act let his government make laws without parliament. Within months, every other party was banned.

26
00:04:53,444 --> 00:05:11,684
In the summer of 1934, he had rivals inside his own movement murdered, along with old enemies. When Hindenburg died that August, Hitler merged the offices of president and chancellor. He was now the Führer, and the army swore an oath to him personally.

27
00:05:13,884 --> 00:05:25,297
Persecution began at once and grew step by step. Jews were pushed out of jobs. In 1935, the Nuremberg Laws stripped them of citizenship.

28
00:05:26,397 --> 00:05:39,347
In November 1938, on Kristallnacht, synagogues were burned and Jewish shops smashed across the country. Around thirty thousand Jewish men were sent to concentration camps.

29
00:05:40,447 --> 00:05:48,852
All the while, a propaganda machine told Germans that their Führer had restored jobs, pride and order. Many believed it.

30
00:05:51,052 --> 00:06:04,279
Next came expansion. Austria was annexed in March 1938. That September, Britain and France agreed at Munich to let him take part of Czechoslovakia, hoping to keep the peace.

31
00:06:05,379 --> 00:06:15,725
It did not work. On the first of September 1939, Germany invaded Poland. Two days later, Britain and France declared war.

32
00:06:16,825 --> 00:06:26,895
Within two years, his armies had conquered most of Europe, from France to Greece. In June 1941, he invaded the Soviet Union.

33
00:06:27,995 --> 00:06:36,955
And in December 1941, after Japan attacked Pearl Harbor, he declared war on the United States as well.

34
00:06:39,155 --> 00:06:45,064
Behind the front lines, the regime carried out the systematic murder of Europe's Jews.

35
00:06:46,164 --> 00:06:57,364
Mobile killing squads shot more than a million people in the occupied east. Then came the death camps: Auschwitz, Treblinka, Sobibor, Belzec, Chelmno, Majdanek.

36
00:06:58,464 --> 00:07:08,363
In January 1942, senior officials met at a villa at Wannsee, near Berlin, to coordinate what they called the Final Solution.

37
00:07:09,463 --> 00:07:15,671
About six million Jews were murdered. Roughly two out of every three Jews living in Europe.

38
00:07:16,771 --> 00:07:27,139
Hundreds of thousands of Roma, disabled people, political prisoners and others were also killed, along with millions of Soviet prisoners of war.

39
00:07:29,339 --> 00:07:37,061
The tide turned at Stalingrad, where an entire German army surrendered in February 1943.

40
00:07:38,161 --> 00:07:53,351
In July 1944, an officer, Claus von Stauffenberg, planted a bomb at his headquarters. It went off, but Hitler survived. Thousands of suspects were arrested and around two hundred were executed.

41
00:07:54,451 --> 00:08:05,160
By April 1945, Soviet troops were fighting in the streets of Berlin. Hitler refused to surrender and blamed the German people for failing him.

42
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:15,241
On the twenty-ninth of April, he married his long-time companion, Eva Braun. The next afternoon, they both took their own lives.

43
00:08:16,341 --> 00:08:20,160
Eight days later, Germany surrendered unconditionally.

44
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:33,901
At Nuremberg, the surviving leaders of his regime were put on trial before an international court. It helped establish that crimes against humanity could be prosecuted.

45
00:08:35,001 --> 00:08:45,796
In 1948, the United Nations adopted the Genocide Convention. Germany, over decades, confronted what had been done in its name.

46
00:08:46,896 --> 00:09:01,360
The hardest lesson of Hitler's story is not that one evil man rose to power. It is how many ordinary people, institutions and politicians helped him get there, believing at each step that it would not go further.

47
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:08,988
It did go further. That is why the world still says his name, and why it still says: never again.
